## 5. What is auto-configuration in Spring Boot, and how does it work?

→ Auto-configuration automatically configures beans based on the libraries available, application properties, and beans already defined.

→ For example, when the required web dependencies are available, Spring Boot can configure the web application infrastructure automatically.

→ It uses conditional annotations such as `@ConditionalOnClass`, `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`, and `@ConditionalOnProperty`.

→ If I define my own bean, a default configuration may back off when its conditions specify that no existing bean should be present.

**Example:**

```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnProperty(
    name = "feature.email.enabled",
    havingValue = "true"
)
class EmailConfiguration {

    @Bean
    EmailService emailService() {
        return new EmailService();
    }
}
